      "math_academy_id": "q-221705",
      "problem": "Disprove the following statement:\n\n$4∣{n}^{3}+4$ for any $n\\in Z.$\n\nTo {{field-1}} a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a {{field-2}}. \nIn other words, we need to show that {{field-3}}\n\n$$\n4{{field-4}}{n}^{3}+4.\n$$\n\nNotice that by substituting $n=$ {{field-5}} into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{3}+4 & =({{field-6}})^{3}+4 \\\\  & ={{field-7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is {{field-8}} by $4.$ \n\nSo, $n=$ {{field-9}} is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.",
